1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:09,618
1932, Australia. An army declared war on birds with machine guns... and lost!

2
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:16,629
November 1932. The endless wheat fields of Western Australia.

3
00:00:17,908 --> 00:00:21,138
A cloud of dust rises on the horizon.

4
00:00:22,492 --> 00:00:27,002
It's not an army. It's thousands of birds.

5
00:00:27,075 --> 00:00:32,645
And in a few days, the Australian army will go to war against them.

6
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:38,026
This is the incredible but true story known as the Great Emu War.

7
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:43,845
The emu is a giant bird found only in Australia.

8
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:51,703
It's almost as tall as a person. It can't fly, but it can run at nearly 50 km/h.

9
00:00:51,908 --> 00:00:54,700
And it's very, very tough.

10
00:00:56,742 --> 00:01:01,881
After the First World War, the Australian government gave land to returning soldiers.

11
00:01:03,617 --> 00:01:07,300
Many of these veterans became wheat farmers in Western Australia.

12
00:01:09,367 --> 00:01:14,700
But in 1929, the Great Depression hit and wheat prices crashed.

13
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:20,977
The farmers were already struggling. Then came an even bigger problem.

14
00:01:22,492 --> 00:01:27,464
Every year after breeding season, emus migrate from the inland toward the coast.

15
00:01:28,825 --> 00:01:33,489
In 1932, they found fields full of water and wheat along the way.

16
00:01:35,617 --> 00:01:40,872
According to sources, around 20,000 emus invaded the farms.

17
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:45,806
They ate and trampled the crops, and knocked down the fences.

18
00:01:46,783 --> 00:01:50,473
And the holes in the fences let rabbits into the fields too.

19
00:01:52,033 --> 00:01:56,171
Desperate, the farmers sent a delegation to the capital to ask for help.

20
00:01:57,742 --> 00:02:03,979
These men had used rifles in the war, and they knew exactly what they wanted: machine guns.

21
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:07,386
Defence Minister George Pearce agreed.

22
00:02:09,033 --> 00:02:15,561
Under Major Meredith, two soldiers, two machine guns and 10,000 rounds were sent.

23
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:20,754
The plan was simple: catch the emus in groups and stop them all at once.

24
00:02:21,908 --> 00:02:24,425
Everyone thought it would be over in a few days.

25
00:02:26,742 --> 00:02:31,793
But right at the start, rain delayed the operation.

26
00:02:31,950 --> 00:02:36,522
Finally, on November 2nd, the soldiers spotted the first flock.

27
00:02:36,992 --> 00:02:41,401
The moment the machine gun fired, the emus scattered.

28
00:02:41,325 --> 00:02:45,675
The flock split into small groups and ran in every direction.

29
00:02:45,908 --> 00:02:51,448
A few days later, the soldiers tried to ambush about a thousand emus at a dam.

30
00:02:51,783 --> 00:02:56,753
Just as they were about to open fire, the machine gun jammed.

31
00:02:56,908 --> 00:02:58,974
The emus escaped again.

32
00:03:01,742 --> 00:03:07,247
Major Meredith had a new idea: he mounted a machine gun on a truck.

33
00:03:08,492 --> 00:03:12,220
But the emus were faster than the truck.

34
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:17,253
The truck bounced so much on the rough ground that aiming was impossible.

35
00:03:19,075 --> 00:03:23,403
According to sources, each flock had a lookout emu standing guard.

36
00:03:24,867 --> 00:03:28,164
When soldiers came close, the flock scattered in a flash.

37
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:37,253
After a week, the army had used thousands of bullets. But the number of emus had barely changed.

38
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:40,461
Newspapers began making fun of the situation.

39
00:03:42,283 --> 00:03:44,916
It was even debated in parliament.

40
00:03:46,742 --> 00:03:48,505
And the army pulled out.

41
00:03:51,325 --> 00:03:59,351
Major Meredith later said: If I had an army with the toughness of these birds, I could face any army in the world.

42
00:04:00,825 --> 00:04:05,234
As the farmers kept complaining, the soldiers came back a few days later.

43
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:11,953
This time they did a little better. But the emus were still in the fields.

44
00:04:12,492 --> 00:04:16,254
In December, the army ended the operation for good.

45
00:04:17,450 --> 00:04:23,025
In the following years farmers asked for help again, but the army never came back.

46
00:04:23,075 --> 00:04:27,733
Instead, the farmers were given a bounty system and stronger fences.

47
00:04:29,408 --> 00:04:35,336
Today, emus still live in Australia. They're even on the country's coat of arms.

48
00:04:36,533 --> 00:04:43,091
And the Great Emu War is remembered as the war that birds won against an army.

49
00:04:43,550 --> 00:04:48,536
So who really won this war: the army or the emus? Let us know in the comments.
